1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,078
Hi, everyone! This is '설악산' [seol-ak-san] (Seoraksan),

2
00:00:03,078 --> 00:00:05,386
ablaze with red autumn leaves.

3
00:00:05,386 --> 00:00:08,002
Today, let's climb with me, Jieun,

4
00:00:08,002 --> 00:00:09,310
and learn Korean!

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,076
Climbing a mountain stirs up all kinds of thoughts.

6
00:00:17,076 --> 00:00:18,880
Curious how to say them clearly?

7
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:28,620
Today's theme is '의견 표현' [ui-gyeon-pyo-hyeon] (expressing opinions) and '설득' [seol-deuk] (persuasion).

8
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:34,670
Pairing a claim with a reason makes your Korean sound far more logical.

9
00:00:36,610 --> 00:00:39,704
Let's study climbing the red-leaf trail step by step,

10
00:00:39,704 --> 00:00:41,630
organizing our thoughts as we go.

11
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:47,326
Remember: a good opinion needs a '근거' [geun-geo] (reason).

12
00:00:47,326 --> 00:00:49,730
The 'why' makes it persuasive.

13
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:56,694
Now,

14
00:00:56,694 --> 00:01:00,119
let's begin today's lesson on this crisp autumn trail.

15
00:01:00,119 --> 00:01:00,500
Ready?

16
00:01:03,970 --> 00:01:08,150
The most common opener: '제 생각에는' [je-saeng-gak-e-neun] (in my opinion).

17
00:01:08,150 --> 00:01:10,880
It signals 'here comes my view'.

18
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:20,286
'제 생각에는, 설악산이 한국에서 가장 아름다워요' — In my opinion,

19
00:01:20,286 --> 00:01:23,900
Seoraksan is the most beautiful in Korea.

20
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:27,148
To state a claim clearly,

21
00:01:27,148 --> 00:01:30,506
use '저는 ~다고 생각해요' [jeo-neun-~-da-go-saeng-gak-hae-yo] (I think ~).

22
00:01:30,506 --> 00:01:33,640
'생각하다' [saeng-gak-ha-da] (to think) is a verb.

23
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:45,533
I think '울산바위' [ul-san-ba-wi] (Ulsanbawi) over there is truly majestic!

24
00:01:45,533 --> 00:01:48,640
It takes your breath away, right?

25
00:01:50,740 --> 00:01:58,610
To agree, use '동의하다' [dong-ui-ha-da]. '저도 그렇게 생각해요' [jeo-do-geu-reot-ge-saeng-gak-hae-yo] or '동의해요' [dong-ui-hae-yo].

26
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:06,986
When you differ, use '반대하다' [ban-dae-ha-da].

27
00:02:06,986 --> 00:02:11,190
The Korean way: add a reason and stay polite.

28
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:18,080
'Good point. But I see it a bit differently'.

29
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:20,780
Respecting the other first keeps it pleasant.

30
00:02:26,370 --> 00:02:29,148
The key word: '왜냐하면' [wae-nya-ha-myeon] (because),

31
00:02:29,148 --> 00:02:33,090
a conjunctive adverb that attaches a reason.

32
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:43,550
'왜냐하면, 공기가 맑기 때문이에요' — because the air is clean.

33
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:49,062
To move from reason to conclusion,

34
00:02:49,062 --> 00:02:51,250
use '따라서' [tta-ra-seo] (therefore).

35
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:04,721
Today's core pattern — claim and reason in one: '저는 ___다고 생각해요,

36
00:03:04,721 --> 00:03:05,550
왜냐하면 ___'.

37
00:03:08,130 --> 00:03:17,884
'-다고' [--da-go] is indirect quotation: '예쁘다' [ye-ppeu-da] becomes '예쁘다고' [ye-ppeu-da-go],

38
00:03:17,884 --> 00:03:21,590
'좋다' [jot-da] becomes '좋다고' [jot-da-go].

39
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:30,452
I think Seoraksan is beautiful,

40
00:03:30,452 --> 00:03:32,440
because the leaves are truly lovely.

41
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:40,524
Hear it? The 'why' sits inside the sentence,

42
00:03:40,524 --> 00:03:42,270
and the listener nods along.

43
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:49,634
I think the cable car is convenient,

44
00:03:49,634 --> 00:03:50,950
because it saves energy.

45
00:03:56,050 --> 00:04:00,950
Make reasons specific: 'the view opens up wide' beats just 'because it's nice'.

46
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:09,843
I think walking is better,

47
00:04:09,843 --> 00:04:11,910
because you can enjoy the leaves slowly.

48
00:04:17,810 --> 00:04:21,603
After reasons, close with '따라서' [tta-ra-seo] : 'Therefore,

49
00:04:21,603 --> 00:04:23,500
I decided to hike up'.

50
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:33,379
Let expression and gesture join your words — a bright face for conviction,

51
00:04:33,379 --> 00:04:35,180
a raised finger for the reason.

52
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,392
Say it after me: 'I think ~, because ~,

53
00:04:40,392 --> 00:04:41,190
therefore ~'.

54
00:04:47,570 --> 00:04:49,792
Keep opinions polite.

55
00:04:49,792 --> 00:04:55,610
Ending with '-요' [--yo] or '-습니다' [--seup-ni-da] softens even a strong claim.

56
00:04:59,640 --> 00:05:01,717
In sum: 'I think ~, because ~'.

57
00:05:01,717 --> 00:05:04,800
One pattern for claim, reason, and persuasion.

58
00:05:10,870 --> 00:05:12,838
Now a short debate.

59
00:05:12,838 --> 00:05:18,327
When '찬성' [chan-seong] (for) and '반대' [ban-dae] (against) go back and forth,

60
00:05:18,327 --> 00:05:21,020
catch each side's reasons.

61
00:05:25,140 --> 00:05:28,500
Today's topic: 'Cable car up Seoraksan, or hike'?

62
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:29,940
Light and fun, right?

63
00:05:35,290 --> 00:05:37,869
In favor: 'I think the cable car is good,

64
00:05:37,869 --> 00:05:41,015
because it saves time and you can enjoy the view'.

65
00:05:41,015 --> 00:05:41,770
Two reasons!

66
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:47,801
Against: 'I think we should walk,

67
00:05:47,801 --> 00:05:50,060
because you feel the leaves up close'.

68
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:57,127
Jot each reason: 'for = saves time,

69
00:05:57,127 --> 00:05:59,010
against = enjoy leaves'.

70
00:06:04,210 --> 00:06:06,264
If a reason resonates,

71
00:06:06,264 --> 00:06:10,280
follow with '저도 그렇게 생각해요' [jeo-do-geu-reot-ge-saeng-gak-hae-yo] (I think so too).

72
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:17,231
Disagree by first acknowledging: 'That's true,

73
00:06:17,231 --> 00:06:19,880
but I think ~'. Then it's a conversation.

74
00:06:25,410 --> 00:06:30,694
Curious? Ask '왜 그렇게 생각하세요' [wae-geu-reot-ge-saeng-gak-ha-se-yo]? (Why do you think so?

75
00:06:30,694 --> 00:06:30,910
).

76
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:37,655
A debate is about learning, not winning.

77
00:06:37,655 --> 00:06:39,708
On a good reason, say 'Ah, I see'!

78
00:06:39,708 --> 00:06:41,700
and be ready to change your mind.

79
00:06:46,370 --> 00:06:47,319
For or against,

80
00:06:47,319 --> 00:06:49,596
a clear reason makes a fine opinion.

81
00:06:49,596 --> 00:06:51,430
What matters is saying 'why'.

82
00:06:57,930 --> 00:06:59,575
Now, persuasion — not force,

83
00:06:59,575 --> 00:07:02,630
but gently moving someone's heart with good reasons.

84
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:09,526
'Just a little more! I think we can reach the top,

85
00:07:09,526 --> 00:07:11,360
because we've made it this far'.

86
00:07:15,530 --> 00:07:18,281
The secret: empathy first, reason second.

87
00:07:18,281 --> 00:07:19,488
'I know it's hard.

88
00:07:19,488 --> 00:07:22,440
But a bit farther, there's a gorgeous view'.

89
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:28,888
In Korea, courtesy matters greatly.

90
00:07:28,888 --> 00:07:30,529
However much you disagree,

91
00:07:30,529 --> 00:07:32,990
respect the other and end with a smile.

92
00:07:39,130 --> 00:07:41,278
With elders, be extra polite.

93
00:07:41,278 --> 00:07:44,240
'May I share my view'? asks leave first.

94
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:50,499
The summit at last!

95
00:07:50,499 --> 00:07:53,580
'I knew we'd make it, because we never gave up'.

96
00:08:01,490 --> 00:08:05,808
Today we learned to open with '제 생각에는' [je-saeng-gak-e-neun],

97
00:08:05,808 --> 00:08:10,680
give a reason with '왜냐하면' [wae-nya-ha-myeon], close with '따라서' [tta-ra-seo],

98
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:13,780
and agree/disagree politely.

99
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:19,990
What's your opinion?

100
00:08:19,990 --> 00:08:22,232
Share it with a reason in the comments.

101
00:08:22,232 --> 00:08:26,660
Let's meet again for logical Korean — and don't forget to subscribe and like!

